WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.459 --> 00:00:06.540
Sequencer is Unreal Engine's multi-track editor used for creating and playing back

2
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.980
cinematic sequences in real time.

3
00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:14.500
It's the backbone for all linear content creation in Unreal Engine and a production

4
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:22.480
hub combining aspects of an editorial department with layout and animation, all within a non-linear

5
00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:23.539
timeline.

6
00:00:23.539 --> 00:00:28.199
Sequencer, while it can be used for more traditional film-like applications, has

7
00:00:28.199 --> 00:00:33.899
many other capabilities that are beneficial to real-time applications, such as video games.

8
00:00:33.899 --> 00:00:38.700
For the purposes of this course, we'll create in-game cutscenes with pre-defined

9
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:45.039
camera angles, animation, and event triggers, however, it's important to note that a sequence

10
00:00:45.039 --> 00:00:49.799
doesn't always have to include cameras, for example, you could use a sequence in-game

11
00:00:49.799 --> 00:00:55.219
to play a building collapse instead of relying on chaos physics at runtime.

12
00:00:55.219 --> 00:01:00.020
It's also important to note that sequences can be loaded and unloaded at real-time and

13
00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:03.090
are independent of a level.

14
00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:07.810
A well-defined framework and pipeline for your cinematics are essential for maintaining

15
00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:11.569
a clean, organized, and accessible project.

16
00:01:11.569 --> 00:01:17.209
A common structure for feature films is the use of a parent cinematic, commonly used

17
00:01:17.209 --> 00:01:22.250
for viewing the entire cinematic as well as rendering the final output, and nested

18
00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:25.189
sequences and shots.

19
00:01:25.189 --> 00:01:30.109
Using a structure like this has many benefits for video game development, specifically,

20
00:01:30.109 --> 00:01:35.090
this type of workflow enables multiple departments to work concurrently on shots

21
00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:38.310
while preserving a well-organized file structure.

22
00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:42.829
And while this structure works for video games and films, I strongly recommend that

23
00:01:42.829 --> 00:01:48.209
you take time early on in your project to define a file and folder structure for

24
00:01:48.209 --> 00:01:53.750
your in-game cutscenes, establish your shot, assembly, and nesting templates, as well

25
00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:58.120
as define a pipeline that best suits your project.

26
00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:02.659
Within our sequences, we have a few options for spawning or controlling various actors.

27
00:02:02.659 --> 00:02:05.859
Specifically, there are three classifications.

28
00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:10.780
Possessibles are actors that already exist in our level that can be included in our

29
00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:15.020
sequence, allowing us to control various parameters for that actor.

30
00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:19.689
For example, within our level, we may have a doorway that we need to have our actor

31
00:02:19.689 --> 00:02:20.930
open.

32
00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:25.650
We can include the door actor, or blueprint, in our sequence and animate the door opening

33
00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:27.090
and closing.

34
00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:31.550
After the sequence is complete, the door will still remain in the level.

35
00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:34.509
Our second type is known as a spawnable.

36
00:02:34.509 --> 00:02:38.669
Spawnable actors are only loaded when the sequence is playing and are removed after

37
00:02:38.669 --> 00:02:40.430
the sequence is complete.

38
00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:44.270
For example, let's say we have a sequence where we have a spaceship fly in to deliver

39
00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:47.550
cargo and then it flies off afterwards.

40
00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:52.629
Because we really only need the ship to appear during the sequence, using a spawnable actor

41
00:02:52.629 --> 00:02:57.189
is the most logical choice as opposed to placing the ship in the level and trying

42
00:02:57.189 --> 00:02:59.969
to control it and then hide it afterwards.

43
00:02:59.969 --> 00:03:05.770
Lastly, introduced in 5.5 is a new type known as replaceables.

44
00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:10.280
Replaceables are similar to spawnables, however they can be swapped at runtime.

45
00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:14.639
For example, let's say that you have a game where players have the ability to customize

46
00:03:14.639 --> 00:03:18.680
a character at any time throughout the game.

47
00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:22.719
Rather than loading a generic actor into our sequence, we want to load the player's

48
00:03:22.719 --> 00:03:27.360
specific character as it appears when the sequence starts.

49
00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.400
By using a replaceable actor in your sequence, you can build and animate the sequence using

50
00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:38.159
a reference actor and replace that reference actor with the player's character at runtime.

51
00:03:38.159 --> 00:03:42.860
The same principle applies to any actor that's loaded into a sequence when it's defined

52
00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:43.819
as a replaceable.
